  "slug": "evolution-sexual-efec",
  "summary": "Seven studies test trait evolution against genomic, demographic and ecological parameters, showing phenotype–diversification links vary across lineages.",
  "synthesis": "These studies relate phenotypic divergence to lineage diversification across plants, birds, lizards, butterflies and insects, and report that the relationship is uneven. Mimosa clades span a continuum of diversification rates, with radiations contributing most diversity and disparity and linked to abiotic niche lability. In island kingfishers, faster plumage color evolution tracks faster diversification and accelerates as genomic diversity declines, consistent with drift. Bark anoles show convergent signaling traits decoupled from genomic species boundaries, with gene flow shaping speciation outcomes. Avian iridescent dichromatism is explained by both Darwinian and Wallacean processes, and leaf-footed bug dimorphism is labile. Masked owls and Heliconius cydno add genome-scale evidence of divergence tied to phenotype, island isolation and Andean geography. The shared advance is testing trait evolution directly against genomic and demographic parameters.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-sonnet-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-25"
}
